s. For C: makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education. For D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, hot temper, know-it-all.\n\nJames could argue that Candidate D's problem solving and responsibility may outweigh B's communication concerns, but D's hot temper and know-it-all may affect crew cooperation. Or argue that Candidate A's anticipation and leadership are strong, but his show-off attitude and closed-mindedness are concerns. He could suggest that we need to discuss strengths of A, C, D before final decision.\n\nHe should state his current recommendation. He could keep B as recommendation but note need to discuss other candidates. Or he could shift to D if he thinks D's strengths are more balanced.\n\nGiven the shared mental model: James hasn't spoken yet, and the panel hasn't covered strengths of A, C, D. He should bring up strengths of A, C, D and perhaps argue that we need to discuss them before final recommendation. He could still vote for B but note that we need more discussion. Or he could change vote to D if he believes D's strengths outweigh B's concerns.\n\nLet's evaluate: B: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical). Concerns: pretentious, wrong tone (communication). Also weak memory for numbers (attention). D: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concerns: hot temper, know-it-all (communication/cooperation). Both have communication concerns. B's memory weakness might affect numeric tasks. D's hot temper may affect crew cooperation more severely. B's pretentiousness may be less severe? Hard 